e = -1\n    best_subset = []\n    # enumerate all subsets\n    from itertools import combinations\n    # Actually we can use bitmask\n    for r in range(len(remaining_indices)+1):\n        for combo in combinations(remaining_indices, r):\n            cost = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in combo)\n            if cost > token_budget - forced_cost:\n                continue\n            value = sum(word_scores[i] for i in combo)\n            if value > best_value:\n                best_value = value\n                best_subset = list(combo)\n    selected_words = sorted(list(forced_set) + best_subset)\n    # Flatten token indices\n    retained_tokens = []\n    for i in selected_words:\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\n    retained_token_indices = sorted(set(retained_tokens))  # maybe sort unique? But could there be duplicates? Assuming unique, but set to be safe. But order? Output schema doesn't specify order, but typical is ascending. So sort.\n    tokens_used = len(retained_tokens)  # or len(retained_token_indices) if unique, but probably tokens are unique so same.\n    return {\n        \"retained_token_indices\": retained_token_indices,\n        \"selected_words\": selected_words,\n        \"tokens_used\": tokens_used\n    }\n\nBut wait: The output definition says \"retained_token_indices\": array of integers, \"selected_words\": array of integers. No requirement for uniqueness or sorting, but likely sorted. I'll sort for consistency.\n\nNow, note that forced_words may contain duplicate indices? The fixture shows [0] only. We'll assume no duplicates. But we can handle by converting to set.\n\nPotential issue: The enumeration using combinations might be slow for large n, but assume small. We could also use DP with backtracking for large n, but given it's a scaffold, likely small.\n\nOne more thing: The output schema uses \"additionalProperties\": false, so our returned dict must have exactly those three keys.\n\nNow we need to output the implementation as a JSON string inside an object with key \"implementation\". We must escape the string properly. So write Python source, then escape newlines etc.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case['word_token_indices']\\n    word_scores = case['word_scores']\\n    forced_words = case['forced_words']\\n    token_budget = case['token_budget']\\n    \\n    n = len(word_token_indices)\\n    forced_set = set(forced_words)\\n    forced_cost = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in forced_set)\\n    remaining_indices = [i for i in range(n) if i not in forced_set]\\n    best_value = -1.0\\n    best_subset = []\\n    # Enumerate subsets of remaining indices\\n    from itertools import combinations\\n    for r in range(len(remaining_indices) + 1):\\n        for combo in combinations(remaining_indices, r):\\n            cost = sum(len(word_token_indices[i]) for i in combo)\\n            if cost > token_budget - forced_cost:\\n                continue\\n            value = sum(word_scores[i] for i in combo)\\n            if value > best_value:\\n                best_value = value\\n                best_subset = list(combo)\\n    \\n    selected_words = sorted(list(forced_set) + best_subset)\\n    retained_tokens = []\\n    for i in selected_words:\\n        retained_tokens.extend(word_token_indices[i])\\n    retained_token_indices = sorted(set(retained_tokens))\\n    tokens_used = len(retained_tokens)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"retained_token_indices\\\": retained_token_indices,\\n        \\\"selected_words\\\": selected_words,\\n        \\\"tokens_used\\\": tokens_used\\n    }\"}\n\nBut need to escape: backslashes, newlines, quotes. Actually in JSON, newline should be \\n, backslash \\\\, quote \\\". We'll produce a properly escaped string.\n\nAlternatively, we can write the Python code in multi-line and then apply json.dumps. But manually we need to escape.\n\nLet's write the code with proper escaping. We'll use